WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.923
LIP firing looks like the running total.

2
00:00:06.723 --> 00:00:09.483
But does the decision actually need LIP?

3
00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:12.697
A recording shows what activity goes with a choice.

4
00:00:13.497 --> 00:00:16.977
Only changing the activity can test whether the choice depends on it.

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.820
MT passed a first test.

6
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.340
Researchers passed weak current into small clusters of MT neurons that shared a

7
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.340
preferred direction.

8
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.000
The monkeys chose that direction more often, as if that motion had been added to the

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.000
dots.

10
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:38.183
Katz, Yates, Pillow and Huk then silenced each area in turn, in rhesus monkeys.

11
00:00:39.283 --> 00:00:44.343
They used muscimol, a drug that activates GABA-A receptors and quiets nearby

12
00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:45.343
neurons.

13
00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:47.247
Predict each outcome.

14
00:00:48.107 --> 00:00:51.867
If LIP is the accumulator, what happens when you silence MT?

15
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:54.087
And when you silence LIP?

16
00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:03.187
Silencing MT impaired motion discrimination.

17
00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:09.787
Silencing LIP produced no measurable impairment of the motion decisions, in the

18
00:01:09.787 --> 00:01:10.787
tasks tested.

19
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.250
Did the injection simply miss?

20
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.470
The same monkeys did a free-choice task, with no right answer.

21
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:24.070
After LIP inactivation, they chose targets in the affected part of space less often.

22
00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:28.443
And their eye movements to that side landed less accurately.

23
00:01:29.203 --> 00:01:30.903
So the drug had acted on LIP.

24
00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:35.037
That control is what makes the null result readable.

25
00:01:36.077 --> 00:01:39.557
Still, a null result cannot rule out other areas compensating.

26
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.400
Other studies did find deficits after parietal inactivation.

27
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:46.600
So the result is debated.

28
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.560
In one study, the biases faded while the area was still silenced.

29
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.320
That suggests other areas compensated.

30
00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.670
Which areas a decision needs remains an open question.

31
00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:04.593
If a decision adds up evidence, the adding happens somewhere, perhaps in several

32
00:02:04.593 --> 00:02:09.833
places. But a neuron that shows the running total is not always the place it is

33
00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:10.093
needed.
